    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d81">

                <pb facs="0177" n="75" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg75"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.34/172: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-07-08T17:00:00+01:00">July 8, 1935—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 8—12:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>304. The Sunday <hi rendition="#italic">Times</hi> carries a story without date
                    or place of origin to the effect that details of the German program of naval
                    construction have been communicated by the Germans themselves direct to France
                    and the other signatories to the Washington and London naval treaties. It would
                    be helpful in my conversation tomorrow with Sir Samuel Hoare<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d81-f1"/> if I could know authoritatively whether the United States
                    Government has received this information from Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d81-f1" n="30" xml:id="d81-f1-content">
                <p>British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, June 7–Deeember 18, 1935.</p>
